@node The Idea
@chapter The Idea

Sheet-music PDFs are pictures. Editing pictures of music is a dead end, and
comparing two of them tells you nothing about @emph{what} changed. chantey
is built on one architectural decision:

@quotation
@strong{The PDF is a rendering, not the source.} One canonical MusicXML
file per piece is the single source of truth; audio and notation are both
derived from it.
@end quotation

Everything follows from that. An edit is a change to the canonical file,
so every artifact regenerates consistently. A diff is meaningful, because
two versions of the same piece differ in identifiable measures. And the
comparison page can highlight exactly those measures, because the tool ---
not a human --- computed them.

chantey supplies the pipeline: ingest, inspection, rendering, diffing, and
the comparison page. The musical @emph{judgment} --- what to change ---
stays with people and the scripts they request (@pxref{Editing Scores}).

@node Setup
@chapter Setup

chantey wraps existing engines. Each is checked when needed, with an
actionable error if missing:

@table @strong
@item MuseScore + FluidSynth (apt)
@example
sudo apt install musescore3 fluidsynth fluid-soundfont-gm
@end example
PDF engraving and the piano voice.

@item Audiveris (flatpak) --- only for PDF ingest
@example
flatpak remote-add --user --if-not-exists flathub \
    https://dl.flathub.org/repo/flathub.flatpakrepo
flatpak install -y --user flathub org.audiveris.audiveris
@end example
Optical music recognition. Skip it if your scores exist as MusicXML.

@item hornpipe --- the deterministic synth voice
@example
uv tool install git+https://github.com/mr-kelley/hornpipe
@end example

@item sfizz --- only for @option{--engine sfizz}
The sfztools @code{sfizz} package, which provides @command{sfizz_render},
plus whatever SFZ libraries you map to your parts.
@end table

chantey itself carries heavy Python dependencies (music21, Verovio) in its
own environment; install it with
@code{uv tool install git+https://github.com/mr-kelley/chantey}, or run it
from a clone with @code{uv sync}, then @code{uv run chantey}.

@node The Workflow
@chapter The Workflow

@example
chantey ingest tune.mxl              # -> tune.musicxml (canonical)
chantey info tune.musicxml           # the structural map
#   ... apply the requested edit as a music21 script,
#       writing revised.musicxml ...
chantey diff tune.musicxml revised.musicxml
chantey render tune.musicxml
chantey render revised.musicxml
chantey compare tune.musicxml revised.musicxml
@end example

Open @file{compare.html} in a browser: both versions engraved side by
side, @strong{every measure numbered} (long pieces need cheap references),
the changed measures highlighted in crimson in both columns, and players
for all four audio renders.

Two habits pay off. Run @command{info} first, always --- it is both the
``did ingest work'' check and the reference map when phrasing an edit
request. And run @command{diff} after every edit, @emph{before} rendering
--- it confirms the edit touched exactly what was intended.

@node Commands
@chapter Commands

@section info

@example
chantey info @var{score} [--sfz @var{path}]
@end example

Title and composer, parts with measure counts, key and time signatures
with their change points, tempo marks, note count, repeat barlines and
voltas.

Each part's line also gives its length in quarters as rendered, with
repeats expanded, and as written when that differs. A following line gives
the render tail (2 s) in quarters at the final tempo. Together they bound
the offsets an expression sidecar may use (@pxref{Expression}), so a
sidecar can be checked without a render:

@example
part 'Trumpet': 32 measures; 160 quarters as rendered (repeats expanded; 128 as written)
render tail: 2 s = 3.33333 quarters at the final tempo (expression offsets may run to a part's quarters plus this)
@end example

Under each part whose SFZ is known --- its @env{CHANTEY_SFZ_MAP} entry,
else @option{--sfz @var{path}} --- @command{info} prints that SFZ's
keyswitch map, so an articulation can be mapped to the key a
@code{keyswitch} curve sends (@pxref{Expression}) without reading the SFZ
by hand. Keys are MIDI numbers with SFZ note names (middle C is
@code{c4} = 60):

@example
part 'Trumpet': 32 measures; 160 quarters
  sfz: /lib/VPO/trumpet-SEC-KS-C2.sfz
  keyswitches: sw_lokey 36 (c2) to sw_hikey 40 (e2); sw_default 36 (c2)
    sw_last 36 (c2): Sustain
    sw_last 38 (d2): Normal
    sw_last 39 (d#2): Accent
    sw_last 40 (e2): Staccato
@end example

The reader follows @code{<global>}, @code{<master>}, @code{<group>} and
@code{<region>} inheritance, @code{#define} and @code{#include}. If the
file sets @code{note_offset} or @code{octave_offset}, a note says the keys
are shown as written. An SFZ that cannot be read is a warning, not an
error.

@section ingest

@example
chantey ingest @var{file} [-o @var{out.musicxml}] [--force]
@end example

Produces the canonical MusicXML. @file{.mxl}/@file{.musicxml} input is
normalized as-is --- @emph{always prefer this path when it exists}:
lossless, nothing to proof. @file{.pdf} input goes through Audiveris OMR
(headless; chantey handles the flatpak sandbox plumbing) and prints the
proofing reminder (@pxref{OMR and Proofing}). Metadata is carried through;
a missing title is backfilled from the filename.

@section render

@example
chantey render @var{score} [--voice piano|synth|both]
               [--engine fluidsynth|sfizz] [--sfz @var{path}]
               [--fx dry|light|default] [--expression @var{file}] [--no-pdf]
               [-o @var{dir}] [--force]
@end example

Writes, beside the score or in @var{dir}: @file{@var{stem}.piano.wav},
@file{@var{stem}.synth.toml} + @file{@var{stem}.synth.wav}, and
@file{@var{stem}.pdf}. @xref{The Two Voices}, for what the voices are and
the guarantees they share.

@option{--fx} sets the piano voice's FluidSynth effects:

@table @code
@item dry
The default. Chorus and reverb off (@code{-C 0 -R 0}), so a stem carries
no baked-in space for the mix to fight.
@item light
Chorus off and a small, short reverb (@code{-C 0 -o synth.reverb.active=1
-o synth.reverb.level=0.3 -o synth.reverb.room-size=0.3}).
@item default
Nothing added: FluidSynth's own defaults (chorus on, reverb level 0.9),
which is how chantey 0.1 rendered.
@end table

Arguments in @env{CHANTEY_FLUIDSYNTH_OPTS} follow the preset. The setting
in effect is printed on the piano voice's output line, for example
@samp{Wrote tune.piano.wav  [fx: dry]}. The synth voice and the PDF are
unaffected.

@option{--engine} selects the piano voice's engine: @code{fluidsynth} (the
default, everything above) or @code{sfizz}, one SFZ instrument per part.
Under @code{sfizz} the score's MIDI is split into one file per part (that
part's events plus the tempo map, with the same repeat expansion), and each
part is rendered with

@example
sfizz_render --sfz @var{sfz} --midi @var{mid} --wav @var{wav} -s 44100 -b 256 -q 10 --use-eot
@end example

The parts are summed at unity gain. If the sum
would exceed @minus{}0.1 dBFS, the whole mix is scaled down to it and the
factor is printed.

Every part ends at the score's notated end plus 2 s. Without
@option{--use-eot}, sfizz renders until every voice is silent, and sfizz
1.2.3 can lose a note-off (a @code{delay_random} patch over a looped
sample), so a render could run until the disk is full. sfizz_render 1.2.3
ignores an End-of-Track's own delta, so a text event at that tick carries
the tail. Each @command{sfizz_render} also runs under a file-size limit of
twice that length's bytes plus 1 MiB. Reaching it stops the render, and
chantey reports an error naming the part, with nothing written. Working
files go in a @file{chantey-sfizz-*} directory under
@env{CHANTEY_TMPDIR}, else @env{TMPDIR}. It is removed on success, on
error, and on SIGINT, SIGTERM or SIGHUP.

A part's SFZ comes from @env{CHANTEY_SFZ_MAP} by its part-name, or else
from @option{--sfz @var{path}}. A part with neither is an error that names
it; chantey never falls back to FluidSynth. @option{--sfz} without
@option{--engine sfizz} is an error. @option{--fx} and
@env{CHANTEY_FLUIDSYNTH_OPTS} are ignored under @code{sfizz}, and the
output line says so. That line records the engine, the mapping and the
bound:

@example
Wrote x.piano.wav  [engine: sfizz; sfz: Trumpet=/lib/Trumpet.sfz, Tuba=/lib/Tuba.sfz; eot: +2.0 s; cap: 68.1 MB per part]
@end example

@anchor{Expression}
@option{--expression @var{file}} merges per-part controller curves from a
JSON sidecar into the piano voice's MIDI before the engine runs, under
either engine. music21's export carries no controllers, so without them
every held note sounds at one dynamic. Sampled instruments are built around
CC1 (layer crossfade), CC11 (expression) and CC64 (sustain).

@example
@{"parts": @{"Trumpet": @{"bend_range": 200, "curves": [
  @{"cc": 11, "interp": "linear", "points": [[0, 90], [16, 127]]@},
  @{"cc": 64, "interp": "hold", "points": [[0, 0], [4, 127], [7.5, 0]]@},
  @{"bend": true, "interp": "linear", "points": [[32, 0], [32.5, -100]]@}]@}@}@}
@end example

Parts are matched by part-name as @env{CHANTEY_SFZ_MAP} matches them.
Each curve names one kind: @code{cc} (0--119, values 0--127) or
@code{bend} (values in cents within @code{bend_range}, default 200, which
must match the instrument's own range). Points are @code{[offset, value]},
with offsets in quarter lengths on the rendered timeline (repeats
expanded). @code{hold} sends one event per point. @code{linear} sends one
per step between points, the step being 1/32 quarter or 10 ms, whichever is
coarser. The first value holds from 0 and the last holds to the end.
Repeated values are dropped.

Events go on the channels the part's notes use. At a tick shared with the
notes they come before a note-on and after everything else, so a note
sounds with its value. An unknown part-name, a value out of range, an
offset past the part's length plus the 2 s tail, unsorted offsets, or two
curves on one controller for one part stops the render with nothing
written. Under FluidSynth all parts share one file, so a part with curves
that shares a MIDI channel with another part is an error. Give the parts
distinct instruments, or use @option{--engine sfizz}.

A third kind, @code{keyswitch}, picks the recording in a keyswitch SFZ
(VPO's @code{*-KS-*} patches), note by note:

@example
@{"keyswitch": true, "interp": "hold", "points": [[0, 36], [16, 39], [20.5, 40]]@}
@end example

Each value is a MIDI key. At each point chantey sends that key's note-on
(velocity 100, or the curve's @code{"velocity"}, 1--127) and its note-off
one tick later, before any of the part's note-ons at that tick, so the
note there plays the new articulation. A keyswitch is sent at its points
only: before the first, the SFZ's @code{sw_default} plays. A point that
repeats the previous key is dropped. @code{hold} is the only
@code{interp}, and a part has at most one keyswitch curve. A switch key
inside the part's played range (its lowest to highest note) would sound as
a pitch, so it is an error naming the key and the nearest played note.
Keyswitches need @option{--engine sfizz}: under FluidSynth a keyswitch
curve is an error, because a GM SoundFont would play the key.

A fourth kind, @code{time}, moves each of the part's notes by a few
milliseconds, the lean of a section behind or ahead of the beat:

@example
@{"time": true, "interp": "linear", "points": [[0, 12], [64, 12], [68, -5]]@}
@end example

Values are milliseconds, positive later, within @code{time_max} either way
(a part key beside @code{bend_range}: default 60, greater than 0, at most
1000). One time curve per part. Every note-on and note-off moves by the
curve's value at its own written offset, converted to ticks at the tempo
there: @code{hold} takes the last point at or before the offset,
@code{linear} the exact line between points. A note's on and off are read
separately, so its length changes by the difference. Controllers,
keyswitches, program changes and tempi keep their ticks, so a note written
at a keyswitch and moved earlier plays before the switch. Per key, a note
moved before 0 is clamped to 0, a note-off moved past the next note-on of
its key is pulled back to it, and an event moved before the previous event
of its key is raised to it. Under FluidSynth a part with a time curve that
shares a MIDI channel with another part is an error.

The piano line gains a second bracket, and the synth voice, which ignores
the sidecar, says so. Each switch counts as two events there, its note-on
and its note-off. When a part has a time curve the bracket ends
@code{; shifted: @var{s}; clamped: @var{c}}: the note events moved off
their written tick, and those a clamp moved:

@example
Wrote x.piano.wav  [engine: sfizz; ...]  [expression: x.expr.json; parts: 3; events: 1412]
Wrote x.synth.wav  [--expression ignored (piano voice only)]
@end example

Without @option{--expression}, or with only empty curves, the MIDI and
the render are byte-identical to chantey 0.4.0's. A sidecar without a
@code{keyswitch} curve gives the same bytes as under 0.5.0, and one
without a @code{time} curve the same bytes as under 0.6.0.

@section diff

@example
chantey diff @var{original} @var{revised} [-v]
@end example

Measure-by-measure comparison of note content (pitches, positions,
durations), per part. Prints the changed-measure list; @option{-v} adds
per-part detail; identical scores are reported as such (exit 0 either
way).

@section compare

@example
chantey compare @var{original} @var{revised} [-o @var{out.html}] [--force]
@end example

One self-contained local HTML file. Changed measures come from
@command{diff} automatically --- never a hand-maintained list. Audio
players appear for whichever rendered WAVs already sit beside the two
scores, so run @command{render} on both first. Identical scores produce a
notice and no page.

All outputs are non-clobbering; @option{--force} overwrites.

@node Editing Scores
@chapter Editing Scores

Editing is deliberately @emph{not} a chantey command. Real requests arrive
in informal musical language --- ``the figure in the bass: B-flat, B-flat,
C, D in eighths; split all but the first into twinned sixteenths,
everywhere it occurs'' --- and every one reduces to the same primitive:
@emph{match a shape, transform it, report the measures}. That is a short
music21 script, not a frozen command surface. The worked example (the
request above, verbatim from real use):

@example
from music21 import converter, beam
import copy

s = converter.parse("tune.musicxml")
for meas in s.parts[1].getElementsByClass("Measure"):
    notes = [el for el in meas.notesAndRests if el.isNote]
    for i in range(len(notes) - 3):
        run = notes[i : i + 4]
        if any(float(n.duration.quarterLength) != 0.5 for n in run):
            continue
        ps = [n.pitch.midi for n in run]
        if ps[1]-ps[0] == 12 and ps[2]-ps[1] in (1,2) and ps[3]-ps[2] in (1,2):
            for el in run[1:]:              # the first note keeps its eighth
                el.duration.quarterLength = 0.25
                el.beams = beam.Beams()
                meas.insert(float(el.offset) + 0.25, copy.deepcopy(el))
s.write("musicxml", fp="revised.musicxml")
@end example

Note the shape match (an octave leap then two rising steps) rather than
literal pitches --- informal descriptions often name the notes but miss
the octave, and figures recur transposed. Report the changed measures back
(via @command{chantey diff}) before rendering, so a too-eager match gets
caught by a human first.

@node OMR and Proofing
@chapter OMR and Proofing

Optical music recognition is the one lossy step in the pipeline, so it
gets honest treatment. Audiveris performs well on clean, born-digital
engravings, acceptably on good scans, poorly on photos and handwriting.

After any PDF ingest:

@enumerate
@item
Run @command{chantey info} --- structural absurdities (missing parts, zero
notes) show up immediately.
@item
Render the transcription back (@command{chantey render}) and compare
against the original PDF @emph{by eye} (the engraving) and @emph{by ear}
(the piano render).
@item
Fix what matters in the canonical MusicXML. It is the source now; the PDF
is history.
@end enumerate

Known loss classes: tempo and expression text, fingerings (occasionally
misread as dynamics), articulation glyphs. Pitches and rhythms survive
well on clean input.

@node The Two Voices
@chapter The Two Voices

@table @strong
@item piano
music21 writes MIDI; FluidSynth renders it with a General MIDI SoundFont.
Realistic instrument sound --- what you listen to for pleasure and for
judging a chord voicing's color. With @option{--engine sfizz}, sfizz
renders each part through its own multi-sampled SFZ instrument instead.

@item synth
chantey converts the score to a hornpipe score and hornpipe renders it.
Synth-class timbre, but @emph{deterministic}: the same score produces a
bit-identical WAV, every time, which makes renders reproducible and
regressions detectable.
@end table

Two guarantees, both earned from a real defect found during development:

@itemize @bullet
@item
@strong{The voices always agree about form.} Repeats and voltas are
expanded once, identically, for both. (The defect: one voice honored a
chorus repeat, the other flattened it --- 180 vs. 142 seconds of the same
piece.)
@item
@strong{Tempo changes just work.} hornpipe is single-tempo by design, so
the converter resolves every metronome mark itself and emits timing in
absolute seconds. A piece that moves from quarter=100 to quarter=102
mid-score renders correctly in both voices.
@end itemize

The engraved PDF, by contrast, keeps repeat @emph{notation} on the page
--- players read repeats; audio files play them out.

@node Licensed Material
@chapter Licensed Material

Much sheet music is copyrighted --- including modern arrangements of
public-domain tunes (check the fine print; a 1907 song can carry a 2012
arrangement copyright). The rules chantey is built around:

@itemize @bullet
@item
Work privately and locally on lawfully obtained copies. chantey never
publishes, uploads, or hosts anything; the comparison page is a local
file. Sharing decisions belong to you alone.
@item
Keep working scores and everything derived from them in a directory your
version control ignores (a gitignored @file{scratch/}, say), so
licensed material never enters history.
@end itemize

@node Reference
@chapter Reference

@section Environment

@table @code
@item CHANTEY_SOUNDFONT
Path to the General MIDI SoundFont for the piano voice. Default:
@file{/usr/share/sounds/sf2/FluidR3_GM.sf2}.
@item CHANTEY_FLUIDSYNTH_OPTS
Optional extra FluidSynth arguments for the piano voice, split shell-style
and appended after the @option{--fx} preset. chantey forwards them
unchecked; their validity is the caller's concern. Ignored under
@option{--engine sfizz}.
@item CHANTEY_SFZ_MAP
Per-part SFZ instruments for @option{--engine sfizz}:
@samp{@var{part-name}=@var{path};@var{part-name}=@var{path}}. Part names
are the MusicXML @code{part-name} text, matched exactly first, then
case-insensitively. The map wins per part; @option{--sfz} fills the rest.
An entry that matches no part is a warning.
@item CHANTEY_TMPDIR
Directory for @option{--engine sfizz} working files (per-part MIDI and
stems); point it at a big filesystem for long scores. Unset:
@env{TMPDIR}, else the system default. A value naming no directory is an
error.
@end table

@section Exit status

0 --- success (including ``identical scores'' outcomes). 1 --- error:
unparseable score, missing binary, failed external tool, refused
overwrite. 2 --- usage error. 128 + @var{n} --- stopped by signal @var{n}
(SIGINT, SIGTERM or SIGHUP) during an sfizz render, after its working
files were removed.

@section Output naming

For a score @file{@var{stem}.musicxml}: @file{@var{stem}.piano.wav},
@file{@var{stem}.synth.toml}, @file{@var{stem}.synth.wav},
@file{@var{stem}.pdf}, and (from @command{compare}) @file{compare.html}
beside the revised score.

@bye
